      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Several markers of low-grade chronic inflammation are altered in women with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). Neopterin (NEO) is a marker of celullar immunity, and oxidative stress, mainly produced by activated macrophages. We aimed to evaluate the NEO levels in PCOS patients and correlate them with antropometric and biochemical parameters."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study groups consisted of 69 women with PCOS and 46 healthy controls. Both groups were divided into two subgroups according to their body mass index (BMI): <25 = normoweight, >25 = overweight. The clinical and biochemical parameters and serum NEO levels were analyzed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Circulating levels of NEO were significantly (p < 0.001) higher in women with PCOS (normoweight: 15.9 ± 4.7 nmol/l; overweight 13.3 ± 8.1 nmol/l) compared to controls (normoweight: 8.6 ± 2.0 nmol/l; overweight 9.2 ± 1.8 nmol/l) regardless of their weight classes. Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) (p < 0.05), free and total testosterone (p < 0.001) were significantly elevated in women with PCOS compared to controls after controlling for the effect of obesity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Circulating NEO level s are elevated in PCOS independent of body mass index supporting the suggestion of PCOS is a low-grade chronic inflammatory state."
        }
      ]},
